 were early (preliminary) work [S3]; Cancuen lies 20 kilometres south of the site (the site is 20 km north of Cancuen) [S1]; that work found evidence of people living in the area [S3]; the evidence spans the Late Preclassic to the Late Classic [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-shorts-d81-a2-2026-11-12: verifier card_web_verifier:teaser-verify-003 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-shorts-d81-a2-2026-11-12/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Three stelae form the heart of this site (Tres Islas). [SUPPORTED: https://www.mesoweb.com/Simposio/pdf/18/Tomasic_et_al.2005.pdf]; The stelae are Maya stelae. [SUPPORTED: https://www.mesoweb.com/Simposio/pdf/18/Tomasic_et_al.2005.pdf]; An altar, together with the three stelae, forms the heart of the site. [SUPPORTED: https://www.mesoweb.com/Simposio/pdf/17/Tomasic_y_Fahsen.2004.pdf]; The site is small. [SUPPORTED: https://www.mesoweb.com/Simposio/pdf/18/Tomasic_et_al.2005.pdf]; Early work was done around the site and Cancuen. [SUPPORTED: https://www.mesoweb.com/Simposio/pdf/17/Tomasic_y_Fahsen.2004.pdf]; Cancuen lies 20 kilometres south of the site. [SUPPORTED: https://www.mesoweb.com/Simposio/pdf/18/Tomasic_et_al.2005.pdf]; The early work around the site and Cancuen found signs of people from Late Preclassic times. [SUPPORTED: https://www.mesoweb.com/Simposio/pdf/17/Tomasic_y_Fahsen.2004.pdf]; The signs of people run to Late Classic times. [SUPPORTED: https://www.mesoweb.com/Simposio/pdf/17/Tomasic_y_Fahsen.2004.pdf]"}]'::jsonb),
